---
import AboutHeroImage from "../assets/chris-projection.jpg";
import ContentLayout from "../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../consts";
import { personSchema } from "../utils/schema";
import { getSocialImageURL } from "../utils/socialImage";

const schema = personSchema(await getSocialImageURL(AboutHeroImage));
---

<ContentLayout
  title="About Me"
  socialTitle="About Chris Riebschlager"
  description="Web developer in 2000, inventor at an innovation lab, now Director of Creative Technology at Dimensional Innovations. A Kansas City career spent building things people can touch, play and feel."
  {schema}
  heroImage={AboutHeroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Chris Riebschlager performing in front of a colorful projected backdrop"
>
  <p class="lead">
    I never know what to say when people ask me what I do. Maybe we could start
    with what I've done so far.
  </p>
  <h2>The Work Experiences that Shaped Me</h2>
  <section class="experience">
    <div class="job">
      <span class="year">2000</span>
      <div>
        <h3>River City Studio</h3>
        <p class="title">Web Developer</p>
        <p>
          A print shop just learning to sell "the web," where I built sites for
          the Greater Kansas City Community Foundation, the American Royal and
          The Kansas City Star. I learned the way everyone did back then:
          BBEdit, view-source and stubbornness. Flash is where it clicked.
          Live-rendered, procedurally drawn things that felt more like games
          than web pages.
        </p>
        <p class="takeaway">
          <span>What stuck</span> Clients didn't know what to ask for yet, so I learned
          to listen for what they wanted something to <em>feel</em> like, then translate
          that into something I could build.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="job">
      <span class="year">2006</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Bernstein-Rein</h3>
        <p class="title">Interactive Art Director</p>
        <p>
          An old-school ad agency where the interactive team didn't fit the
          usual boxes, so we all shared one title and designed most of our own
          work. I built microsites and ads for McDonald's, Hostess, USAA and
          Farmland, including banner ads that expanded into full mini-games, a
          format the web has sadly forgotten. It was a dream team, like the '95
          Bulls, and I'm still in touch with many of them.
        </p>
        <p class="takeaway">
          <span>What stuck</span> Good UI and UX before anyone called it that, my
          first real backend and data work, and the confidence to walk into a room
          of executives and present.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="job">
      <span class="year">2011</span>
      <div>
        <h3>RKO Workshop</h3>
        <p class="title">Partner &amp; Technical Lead</p>
        <p>
          A nights-and-weekends studio I started with my friends John and Tim,
          because I could feel my career heading off the screen and into
          physical space. Our first big job was for the Nelson-Atkins:
          touchscreen apps that let visitors make their own Monet-inspired
          paintings alongside the reunited <em>Water Lilies</em> triptych. Work for
          the St. Louis Art Museum and the National WWI Museum followed.
        </p>
        <p class="takeaway">
          <span>What stuck</span> Watching strangers crowd around something we built
          and lose themselves in it. That's when I knew what I wanted to do with my
          life. And with only so many nights and weekends, I learned to protect scope.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="job">
      <span class="year">2012</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Barkley</h3>
        <p class="title">Inventor</p>
        <p>
          Yes, my actual job title was "Inventor." At Moonshot, Barkley's
          innovation lab, we built fast prototypes to pitch clients and win new
          business: a slot car track you drove with your brain (an EEG headset's
          "attention" reading set the speed), a vending machine that paid out
          drinks for social likes, and a portrait booth for SXSW and TEDxAustin
          that rebuilt your photo out of a word you chose, or your own most-used
          tweets. I'm also a named inventor on a patent filing for a smart
          shopping cart that guided you through the store and checked you out
          without a cashier.
        </p>
        <p class="takeaway">
          <span>What stuck</span> A prototype doesn't have to be perfect to matter;
          its job is to get people excited to build. I also left hungry to see something
          all the way through.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="job">
      <span class="year">2018</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Kansas City Art Institute</h3>
        <p class="title">Adjunct Professor</p>
        <p>
          Two nights a week, after full days at DI, I taught three-hour studios
          in creative coding and interactive installation. My students came from
          fabric, ceramics and painting as often as from tech. One
          projection-mapped scans of his own painting back onto the canvas.
          Another built a tiny room with a party inside, where the lights and
          music cut out the moment you opened the door, like you'd killed the
          vibe.
        </p>
        <p class="takeaway">
          <span>What stuck</span> Meet people where they are. Keeping a ceramicist
          and a coder equally engaged taught me to give direction that makes sense
          to the person hearing it. It's how I lead today.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="job">
      <span class="year">2015–now</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Dimensional Innovations</h3>
        <ol class="title progression">
          <li>Software Developer</li>
          <li>Software Development Manager</li>
          <li>Director of Creative Technology</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          I joined as DI's first on-staff software developer, and within two
          months I was at Cleveland's ballpark installing my first large-scale
          games. Kids raced a light-up version of their favorite player to first
          base, and a Kinect scored how well they matched big-league batting
          stances. Since then I've led tech on LEGOLAND's Ferrari attractions
          across three countries, the Amelia Earhart Hangar Museum and <a
            href="/projects/atlas9">Atlas9</a
          >, a 46,000 sq ft immersive world running on nearly 60 codebases.
          Creative Technology has grown to about 25 people.
        </p>
        <p class="takeaway">
          <span>What stuck</span> Going from writing code to leading the people who
          write it was the hardest shift of my career, and it's taught me the most.
          DI is where everything before it came together: tech that people can touch,
          play and feel in real spaces.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <p>
    <strong
      >Want to see what we've built? Check out my <a href="/projects"
        >projects</a
      >.</strong
    >
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong
      >Want to get in touch? Email me at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}
        >{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
      >.</strong
    >
  </p>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .lead {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }
  .experience {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }
  .job {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 88px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-5);
    padding: var(--space-6) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }
  .year {
    padding-top: 2px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--neon-lime);
  }
  .job h3 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .job p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .job .takeaway {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--neon-lime);
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }
  .takeaway span {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--neon-lime);
  }
  .job .title {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    padding: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  .progression {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    list-style: none;
  }
  .progression li {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .progression li + li::before {
    content: "→";
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--neon-lime);
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .job {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-2);
    }
  }
</style>
